Finite intervals have a constructive predecessor escape f(t)=min(t)-1, so the shifted diagonal collision is avoided in every tested interval.  Forbidding that predecessor boundary collapses the toy immediately.  This is the finite shadow of why endpoint/no-endpoint embedding matters in k-critical linear-order diagonalization.

